{% set local_problem %}
<p>Athens' age is one of its greatest assets — historic character, established neighborhoods, mature trees, homes with stories to tell. But age comes with maintenance challenges. Fascia boards exposed to Alabama's humidity for 40, 50, or 60+ years don't last forever, especially when gutters start failing and water runs where it shouldn't.</p>

<p>We see this constantly throughout Athens: homeowners call about new gutters, but when we inspect, we find fascia that's soft, water-stained, or visibly rotting behind existing gutters. The damage often started years ago when gutters began leaking or overflowing, sending water behind them to soak into wood. Alabama's humidity doesn't let that wood dry — it stays damp, creating perfect conditions for rot, mold, and wood-destroying insects.</p>

<p>The <a href="/recent-projects/">neighborhoods around Athens State University, near downtown, and throughout central Athens</a> are particularly affected. These areas have Limestone County's oldest housing stock, with original fascia that's seen decades of Alabama weather. Even homes looking well-maintained from the street often have hidden damage waiting behind their gutters.</p>

<p><strong>The uncomfortable truth:</strong> About 45% of gutter replacement jobs in Athens need some fascia work. Not the whole house — usually isolated sections where gutters leaked or overflowed consistently. Athens' older housing stock means higher rates than newer subdivisions. We'd rather tell you upfront than install new gutters on compromised wood that won't hold them.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>We start with thorough inspection — checking fascia and soffit for water damage, soft spots, and rot. We'll show you exactly what we find and explain what needs repair. No hidden damage, no surprise charges.</p>

<p><strong>For damaged sections:</strong> We carefully remove existing gutters, cut out rotted wood back to solid material, and install new fascia boards. We use quality primed lumber or composite materials designed for exterior exposure in humid climates — materials that hold up to decades of Athens weather.</p>

<p><strong>Material choice matters:</strong> Standard pine fascia lasts maybe 10-12 years in Alabama's humidity before showing moisture damage. Composite or treated lumber lasts 20+ years. The upgrade costs 30-40% more but more than doubles the lifespan. For Athens' older homes, where you want work to last, it's worth it.</p>

<p>Everything gets properly sealed, caulked, and prepped for new gutter installation. Then we install your new seamless gutters on fresh fascia — properly attached with hangers at 24-inch intervals, properly sloped for drainage, designed to prevent the overflow that caused the rot originally.</p>

<p><strong>What we won't do:</strong> Slap new gutters on damaged fascia just to close the sale. Some gutter companies ignore fascia problems because they don't do carpentry. We'd rather lose a job than install gutters we know won't last.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
<p>We've repaired fascia and installed gutters throughout <a href="/recent-projects/">Athens and Limestone County</a> for over 20 years. We understand these jobs are inseparable — you can't have reliable gutters without solid fascia to mount them, and you can't protect fascia without properly functioning gutters keeping water away.</p>

<p>Our crews handle both carpentry and gutter installation. Better coordination, cleaner work, single point of accountability. We've worked on Athens homes of every era — historic downtown residences to mid-century neighborhoods to modern construction — and understand the specific challenges each presents.</p>

<p><strong>Warning from experience:</strong> If someone offers to install new gutters and says your fascia "looks fine" without actually getting up there and pressing on it, get a second opinion. We've replaced plenty of gutters installed 2-3 years ago on damaged fascia — the original installer either didn't notice or didn't mention it. Now the homeowner pays twice.</p>

<p><strong>Our advice:</strong> If you're replacing gutters in Athens and your existing ones have leaked for years, expect some fascia damage. Just the reality of older homes in Alabama's climate. Better to know about it and fix it now than discover it when your new gutters start sagging.</p>
{% endset %}
